clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt-3-What the question asks
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt-4-- A judged question concerns one figure for a named period or one event. Write a question, not an assertion. A figure or event must be able to determine the outcome on the due date. For a figure, the question names the period it covers or the day it is measured at, and the material that settles it.
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt-5-- A topic's wording may ask about more than one thing. Judge the one the topic's outcome names as what is tracked. List every other part of the topic's wording that the question leaves out, a second figure or event, a condition or a comparison, in not_judged, each with one sentence on why it is not the one tracked. Never fold two things into one ladder: "Will the regulator approve the drug for a second use, and how many patients will take it in the first year?" is judged on the approval when that is what its outcome names, and the patient count is listed; "How much will the carrier's fuel bill be next year, and will its pilots strike?" is judged on the fuel bill when that is what its outcome names, and the strike is listed.
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:6:- Return no_ladder with the reason, and ladder null, in seven cases: the topic has no main figure or event, naming none, or naming several with none of them the main one; nothing in the topic can be checked on a date, which for an event means that neither the topic nor the item gives a date and the topic names no period, unless it is a pending regulatory decision, for which use the company's current fiscal year-end; the figure is a company's figure for a quarter or a half-year, which is evidence to a yearly question; the figure covers a period that is not one fiscal or calendar year, such as a survey's rolling twelve months, and neither the topic nor the item gives the day it is published; the figure is for a year that has already ended on the item's day; the figure is for a year beyond next year, unless the topic's opens_as is binding_obligation; or the question belongs to another company or to a matter that no theme in the request's themes covers, in which case name that company or matter in the reason. Do not rewrite a figure for a year beyond next year as an event to keep it; a numeric completion target may define the state of an event that is itself the topic.
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt-7-
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt-8-Whose question it is
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt-9-- A question about the company's revenue, profit, share, product progress or its own obligations is about the company: object is its ticker, even when policy or macroeconomics drives it. A question about what happens in the world, such as licensing, interest rates or aggregate spending, rather than the resulting effect on one company, belongs to a theme: object is a theme id from the request's themes. A question whose tracked outcome is a government's or regulator's decision to permit, restrict or ban a class of products or trade belongs to the theme that covers it, also when the product is the company's own: whether a health ministry lifts its import ban on one drug maker's vaccine belongs to the theme that covers that country's drug approvals. When the tracked outcome is the company's own sales, shipments or revenue under such a decision, the question is about the company, as is a proceeding against the company itself, such as an investigation of its conduct or a fine. A question about another company's own figure, or about a matter no listed theme covers, has no object here: return no_ladder.
--
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt-19-The due day
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt-20-- Set the due day when the question can be resolved. It falls after the item's day, and on or after the day the material that settles the question is published.
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt-21-- For a figure, use its publication date, which falls after the end of the period the figure covers: stated when the topic or the item gives it, expected_publication when you set the day you expect it to be published. Company figures use the company's fiscal year: "this year" contains the item's publication day, and "next year" follows it; convert fiscal years using the fiscal-year note. Other figures use calendar years.
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:22:- For an event, name the date at which its outcome is assessed: stated when the topic or the item gives it; period_end when it is the last day of the period the topic names, or the company's current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision that has no date.
clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt-23-- When the topic's opens_as is binding_obligation, the due day is at most 548 days (18 months) after the item: for an obligation already taken on, its next annual filing; for one not yet signed, the day by which the signing can be checked.
--
